506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 209 0 R
/T (k = 2 likely reflects a broad severity axis \(e.g., relatively healthier vs sicker patients\). That split may be statistically cleaner but is too coarse for our clinical question. We therefore chose k = 4 as a pragmatic balance between subgroup granularity, sample size, and clinical interpretability, allowing us to examine heterogeneous CXR benefit across distinct phenotypes.)
/E (k = 2 likely reflects a broad severity axis \(e.g., relatively healthier vs sicker patients\). That split may be statistically cleaner but is too coarse for our clinical question. We therefore chose k = 4 as a pragmatic balance between subgroup granularity, sample size, and clinical interpretability, allowing us to examine heterogeneous CXR benefit across distinct phenotypes.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [16]
>>
